-- title:
--   Freeness over ℤ of the anemic Hecke algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, nonzero, let $k$ be an integer, and let $S$ be an arbitrary set of natural numbers. Assume the integral-structure hypothesis [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6): the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule [`CuspForm.intLattice N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) — itself the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of those cusp forms of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients [`ModularFormClass.qCoeff f n`](def/FLTPrelim_Modularity.html#L19) are rational integers — is the whole space $\mathrm{CuspForm}(\Gamma_0(N), k)$. Assume also $1 \le k$. The conclusion is that [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N k S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}$ of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathrm{CuspForm}(\Gamma_0(N), k))$ generated by the operators `heckeTLin` attached to primes $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators `heckeULin` attached to primes $q$ with $q \mid N$ and $q \notin S$, is a free $\mathbb{Z}$-module. No rank is asserted, and the excluded set $S$ is unconstrained, so the statement covers all the anemic variants obtained by discarding the operators at the places in $S$.
--
--   This is the standard structural fact that the integral Hecke algebra acting on cusp forms of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$, with the operators at the places of $S$ omitted, is a lattice: a free $\mathbb{Z}$-module. It is used wherever a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of the Hecke algebra is required, in particular in the construction of normalised eigenforms congruent to a given one modulo a maximal ideal and in the statements about integrality of the $q$-coefficients of eigenforms.
